  "query_preview": "..\n\nA much drier airmass is in place over southeast Michigan today, as \nthe 12z DTX sounding checked in with a PW value of 0.72 inches and \n28 C dew point depression at 700 MB. The 850-700 MB Theta-E trough \nwill mostly hold in place through Wednesday morning, as southeast \nMichigan lies in between the moisture axis from the advancing cold \nfront to the west and the large upper-level low/Atlantic moisture \nover southeastern United States. Ultimately, the moisture axis with \nthe cold front will swing through Wednesday night into Thursday as \nbroad trough axis over the Northern Plains swings through the Great \nLakes region Thursday into Friday. Timing, of course, plays a key \nrole in how much instability/CAPE will be in place to support \npossible thunderstorms. At this point, CAPE/bulk shear profiles are \nnot overly impressive and remain on the lower side, so expecting \nmainly non-severe storms with perhaps a marginal risk at best if \noptimal timing works out. Regardless, euro ensembles still show high \nprobability (~70 percent) of a tenth of an inch of more of qpf in \nthe Wednesday evening through Thursday evening time frame as the \nfront slowly tracks from northwest to southeast. Even if the surface \nfront slips just south of the Michigan border by Friday morning, a \nstronger shortwave lagging behind could still be sufficient to \ntrigger additional showers on Friday south of M-59. Both 12z \nCanadian and Euro are showing similar solutions with this shortwave. \n\nNot much to note with respect to temperatures, as highs reach into \nthe 80s each day through the weekend, with mins mainly in the upper \n50s to mid 60s (outside of tonight when mins fall into the 50s).",
